    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#3",
      "text1": "I don't understand what each number means.",
      "text2": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#3",
      "text1": "Tab 1c shows rationale performances however these numbers are quite low compared with prior works (e.g. Paranjape's work at EMNLP 20).",
      "text2": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.",
      "reliability1": "No",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": "Inexpert Statement",
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#3",
      "text1": "And not sure if rationale performances are based on token or sentence selection.",
      "text2": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#4",
      "text1": "This paper has limited coverage on these topics.",
      "text2": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG.",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#4",
      "text1": "Touching different approaches is important here since the way ProtoTrex handle explanation might not easily extend to all other cases.",
      "text2": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG.",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#3",
      "reviewer2": "review#4",
      "text1": "The training loss of the model seems to be too complicated. In Eq.1, there are 6 parts for the overall training loss, and each of them associates with a lambda term (I checked the Appendix, but failed to find how you set lambda terms). Each of them sounds reasonable, but unfortunately, there is no specific ablations on how these parts attribute to the final prediction/explanation.",
      "text2": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).",
      "reliability1": "No",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": "Inexpert Statement",
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#3",
      "reviewer2": "review#4",
      "text1": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss).",
      "text2": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#3",
      "reviewer2": "review#4",
      "text1": "How to determine these hyperparameters?",
      "text2": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 86,
      "reviewer1": "review#3",
      "reviewer2": "review#4",
      "text1": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams.",
      "text2": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 88,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#3",
      "text1": "According to Table 1, activated parameters are made fixed around 1B, however, it might not be clear about computational cost in this work's setting.",
      "text2": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days).",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 88,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#3",
      "text1": "The paper does not provide a fair comparison by fixing total parameters but ignore the computational cost (FLOPs) or activated parameters. In traditional MoE research, the general goal is to achieve better quality with a fixed computational cost (FLOPs), not with a fixed total parameters. The reviewer understand that this method provides a efficient way saving total parameters, but the reviewer suspects to achieve better quality, this method would also significantly increase the FLOPs compared to traditional top1 or top2 based routing used in switch transformer and GLaM.",
      "text2": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days).",
      "reliability1": "No",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": "Misunderstanding",
      "error_type2": null
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 88,
      "reviewer1": "review#2",
      "reviewer2": "review#3",
      "text1": "The proposed method is a minor improvement over traditional parameter sharing scheme like suggested in Universal Transformer.",
      "text2": "In addition, this paper proposes to have a fixed number of global MoE layers, which is probably not suitable when a pre-training dataset has very diverse tokens.",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "No",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": "Neglect"
    },
    {
      "paper_idx": 89,
      "reviewer1": "review#1",
      "reviewer2": "review#2",
      "text1": "In other words, I'd argue that with or without recitation, the objective remains the same (i.e., masked or next-word prediction), so this motivation to me feels a bit mis-directed.",
      "text2": "(3) Insufficient rationale to support gains and no gains",
      "reliability1": "Yes",
      "reliability2": "Yes",
      "error_type1": null,
      "error_type2": null
    }
  ],
